            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest notable news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover captured a first-of-its-kind image sequence from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. NASA/JPL published the update on Aug. 5, 2026, noting the Mastcam-Z images were taken on July 2, 2026, during sol 1,907 of the mission. In the sequence, Earth appears as a tiny bright dot and is briefly occulted by Phobos—described by a mission scientist as a “unique Earth self-portrait” with a “Phobos photobomb”【5:0†source】.\n\nA recent milestone before that: Perseverance completed a “marathon” on Mars, reaching 26.2 miles / 42.195 km of driving on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months on the Red Planet. NASA said it reached that distance much faster than Opportunity did, and Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter imagery captured the rover and its tracks from orbit around the same time【5:3†source】.\n\nNASA’s rover image archive also shows ongoing activity, with an “Image of the Week” for mission Week 285, covering July 26–Aug. 1, 2026【5:1†source】.",
                "annotations": [
                  {
                    "type": "url_citation",
                    "url": "https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/news/nasas-perseverance-rover-watches-earth-vanish-behind-martian-moon/",
